PROBLEM:
Suppose we have three systems, apple, android, laptop. And we have a weather forecast API, now all three systems
call the API again and again after every minute, is there a change in weather?is there a change in weather?(This
calling an API again and again is called pooling).
When a system is doing polling than in a small system it is easy to initiate the calls again and again but in a large
system it is difficult to do that.
Problem is when an object change its state than traditionally, we make manual calls to the related method of a class
one by one. This causes tight coupling and makes it difficult to dynamically add or remove listeners without modifying
core code.

And so a new pattern is introduced named as observer design pattern.

SOLUTION (Observer Design Pattern):
The main object (Subject) maintains a list of Observer objects (subscribers) that implement a shared interface. Whenever
a state change happens, the Subject loops through this list and invokes a method (like update()) on each subscribed object
automatically.
To generate a event two type of terms are needed:
1) Publisher: Publisher are the classes that will generate the event.
2) Subsriber: Subscriber are the classes which wab=nts to know that event has happened.

So when any change in weather-API is happened, automatically the call is generated to iphone users,android users, windows users.
Where the change is happened that is called Subject.
Where the change has to notified that are called Observers.
This is a pub-sub model.

Definition:
Observer design pattern defines one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object(known as
Subject) change its state, all its dependents(Observer) are notified automatically.

TIP:
- Use Facade when you want to hide complexity behind a simple front wall.
- Use Observer when you want to broadcast changes to listening objects without locking them together.
- Subjects never change the state of observers. It only tells about its own state was changed.